    intro: "We built this track with two former National Geographic expedition guides — one who has spent 22 years in the Mara, one who knows the Samburu riverbed hide positions better than anyone alive. The result is a photography itinerary that treats light and position as the primary variables, not the camp breakfast time.",
    why: "Kenya's photography window is the most democratic on the continent. You don't need a 600mm lens to get the shot that makes people ask 'where was that taken?'. The Mara's open savannah gives you proximity and context simultaneously. The Samburu's dusty red palette is unlike anything in East Africa. And the light — particularly July to October — is the kind that photographers spend careers chasing.",
